INT. GRAIN SILO - CERES MINING COLONY - NIGHT CYCLE

A vertical steel cylinder, dim and echoing. Frost on the catwalks. The grain gauge reads EMPTY in red.

COMMANDER ABEL TRASK, 50s, in a patched thermal jacket, climbs the ladder to the upper platform. His breath fogs. His hand finds a rusted rail.

MOSES KWAME, 30s, the colony engineer, waits at the top with a lantern and a datapad. He hasn't slept.

TRASK
You sent for me.

KWAME
I sent for the Commander. Not the man who made the ration cuts.

TRASK
There is no difference.

KWAME
There was yesterday.

Trask steps onto the platform. The grating hums under his boots.

TRASK
Show me.

Kwame lifts the lantern. Across the platform, a hatch stands open, stamped CORE STORAGE - SEALED. Inside, rows of crates. Full. Stacked to the ceiling.

Trask stares. His face doesn't change. He does not look at Kwame.

KWAME
Four hundred tons. Forty days of food. Sealed since the council declared the shortage.

TRASK
Who else knows?

KWAME
Nobody.

TRASK
Then keep it that way.

Kwame lowers the lantern. He steps between Trask and the hatch.

KWAME
You knew.

TRASK
I signed the seal.

KWAME
People have been rationing since spring.

TRASK
People have been alive since spring.

KWAME
My daughter has stopped growing.

Trask finally looks at him.

TRASK
He is not the first to say that to me.

Kwame's hand tightens on the datapad. He raises it as if to throw. Trask doesn't flinch. Doesn't move.

KWAME
Open it. Tonight.

TRASK
And in forty days?

KWAME
We'll have forty days.

Trask takes a slow breath. Reaches for the keypad. Kwame watches his hand. Trask enters a code. The lock chunks open.

He shoves the door wide. A cold wind of old grain sweeps over them. Kwame stumbles. Trask catches his arm.

For a moment, he holds on. Then he lets go, and he doesn't look back.
